WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.719
A valley you can walk through, where every cloud, mountain and blade

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:05.353
of grass is drawn in code.

00:00:05.733 --> 00:00:09.130
Claude Fable 5.2 helped build it, and it lives on a

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.365
new platform called patch.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:13.789
Hi, I'm Mossby!

00:00:13.789 --> 00:00:16.786
The platform first, then both scenes, shader by shader.

00:00:20.242 --> 00:00:24.207
patch launched on November the ninth: a command line tool and an

00:00:24.207 --> 00:00:28.502
MCP server for coding agents, an asset library, and a host for games.

00:00:28.882 --> 00:00:31.203
Its tools are search, git, release and test.

00:00:31.203 --> 00:00:34.394
None of them makes a mesh, a texture or a sound.

00:00:34.394 --> 00:00:36.135
Your agent still writes the game.

00:00:37.592 --> 00:00:40.984
The library is real, and most of it is Kenney.

00:00:40.984 --> 00:00:44.716
Every item is public domain or Creative Commons with attribution, and

00:00:44.716 --> 00:00:46.073
carries its credit line.

00:00:47.892 --> 00:00:49.194
Publishing is one command.

00:00:49.194 --> 00:00:52.447
Your agent builds the game with whatever it already has.

00:00:52.847 --> 00:00:56.682
patch checks the files for secrets, size, and requests to other hosts.

00:00:57.082 --> 00:01:00.737
Patch Desktop plays it once on your own Windows PC and records it.

00:01:01.137 --> 00:01:04.810
Your agent watches that recording and says whether it starts and plays.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.365
Then a malware scan, and an AI model reads the code.

00:01:08.365 --> 00:01:10.660
With no warnings, no person looks at it.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:11.914
And it is live.

00:01:14.692 --> 00:01:17.562
Three things in the docs and terms are worth reading first.

00:01:17.982 --> 00:01:20.399
A new repo starts under the Patch licence.

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:24.025
You can read and remix it, but not take it off patch.

00:01:24.025 --> 00:01:25.536
Both scenes chose MIT instead.

00:01:25.956 --> 00:01:29.346
The terms say that for some games, patch starts the game with

00:01:29.346 --> 00:01:32.171
its anti-cheat off for that session, so mods can load.

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:36.380
And the installer writes a use patch section into your global Claude

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.221
and Codex instructions, then restores it on every update.

00:01:40.742 --> 00:01:44.319
Painted Valley is plain three dot js on WebGL two.

00:01:44.319 --> 00:01:48.611
It downloads 12.8 megabytes, and the scenery in it is code: the

00:01:48.611 --> 00:01:51.472
only pictures are two textures for the footpaths.

00:01:51.852 --> 00:01:53.728
The only model is the walker.

00:01:53.728 --> 00:01:57.480
The grass, trees, clouds, mountains and village are all written in code.

00:01:58.992 --> 00:02:00.737
The finish is a Kuwahara filter.

00:02:00.737 --> 00:02:04.228
Here is a patch of the frame as pixels: two paints meeting

00:02:04.228 --> 00:02:05.682
at an edge, both speckled.

00:02:06.102 --> 00:02:10.833
Around each pixel, take a small window and cut it into four overlapping quadrants.

00:02:11.253 --> 00:02:12.982
Measure how much each quadrant varies.

00:02:12.982 --> 00:02:16.441
The calmest sits wholly on one side of the edge, and the

00:02:16.441 --> 00:02:17.594
pixel takes its mean.

00:02:18.014 --> 00:02:18.984
Do that everywhere.

00:02:18.984 --> 00:02:22.862
Inside a region all four quadrants agree, so the speckle goes flat.

00:02:23.614 --> 00:02:27.763
At the edge, one quadrant always stays on one side, so the edge survives.

00:02:27.763 --> 00:02:29.541
It looks like a loaded brush.

00:02:31.242 --> 00:02:32.577
In the valley, press P.

00:02:32.577 --> 00:02:34.445
Without the finish, the bushes are speckle.

00:02:34.825 --> 00:02:36.938
With it, they are a few flat patches.

00:02:36.938 --> 00:02:40.107
The walker writes a low alpha, so the filter leaves him alone.

00:02:41.692 --> 00:02:44.131
Light on the walker is a step, not a gradient.

00:02:44.551 --> 00:02:48.449
Smoothstep rises from zero to one as the surface turns toward the sun.

00:02:48.869 --> 00:02:51.786
Over a tiny width, so each part of him is two tones

00:02:51.786 --> 00:02:53.001
with a clean turn between.

00:02:53.421 --> 00:02:55.385
The small offset keeps grazing light lit.

00:02:55.385 --> 00:02:58.752
And the shade is a colour of its own, deeper and bluer,

00:02:58.752 --> 00:03:00.155
not the lit one darkened.

00:03:01.742 --> 00:03:03.438
The hour is one number.

00:03:03.438 --> 00:03:07.169
Sky, clouds, mountains and grass all read the same keyed colours,

00:03:07.169 --> 00:03:08.526
so nothing is repainted.

00:03:15.242 --> 00:03:18.236
The second scene is a harbour town in coloured pencil.

00:03:18.236 --> 00:03:20.632
Its claim is pure code, and it holds.

00:03:20.632 --> 00:03:23.926
The only file it loads is a skeleton with its clips.

00:03:24.306 --> 00:03:26.376
The strokes belong to each surface.

00:03:26.376 --> 00:03:30.517
Every vertex carries two numbers, and strokes run along their level lines,

00:03:30.517 --> 00:03:31.897
so they never swim.

00:03:33.242 --> 00:03:35.177
Only their spacing follows the screen.

00:03:35.177 --> 00:03:39.048
The shader measures how fast the field changes per pixel and picks

00:03:39.048 --> 00:03:40.660
a spacing in whole octaves.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.790
Walk in and new strokes fade in half-way between the old ones.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:45.415
Nothing slides.

00:03:45.415 --> 00:03:47.603
Strokes are only added or taken away.

00:03:49.192 --> 00:03:52.100
So the two scenes put their style in different places.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.008
The valley paints the screen, and inks only its walker.

00:03:55.428 --> 00:03:58.984
The town draws on the surfaces themselves, and turns light into how

00:03:58.984 --> 00:04:00.465
hard each stroke is pressed.

00:04:02.142 --> 00:04:05.533
Separate light from paint, then decide where the style lives: on the

00:04:05.533 --> 00:04:06.946
screen, or on the surface.

00:04:10.192 --> 00:04:12.017
patch is plumbing around your agent.

00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:15.666
The valley paints the screen, and the town draws on the surfaces.

00:04:15.666 --> 00:04:18.099
Both are open, so go and walk them.

00:04:18.099 --> 00:04:19.011
Thanks for watching!

00:04:19.431 --> 00:04:21.523
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:21.523 --> 00:04:22.120
I'm Mossby.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:22.419
Bye!
